  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{2t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{\u2033}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t})-4({u}^{\u2032}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t})+4(u{e}^{2t}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t}-4{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{2t}-8u{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{e}^{2t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2033}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{2t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{2t}({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot t=t{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{4t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2t}+Bt{e}^{2t}.\n$$",
